{"name":"South Pacific","hex":"#698694","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"South Pacific","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The southern part of the Pacific Ocean, from the equator to the Antarctic Ocean","The islands of Oceania"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/South_Pacific"}],"entries":[{"title":"South Pacific (musical)","blocks":[{"text":"South Pacific is a musical composed by Richard Rodgers, with lyrics by Oscar Hammerstein II and book by Hammerstein and Joshua Logan. The work premiered in 1949 on Broadway and was an immediate hit, running for 1,925 performances. The plot is based on James A. Michener's Pulitzer Prize–winning 1947 book Tales of the South Pacific and combines elements of several of those stories. Rodgers and Hammerstein believed they could write a musical based on Michener's work that would be financially successful and, at the same time, send a strong progressive message on racism."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6b/Musical1949-SouthPacific-OriginalPoster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Pacific_(musical)"},{"title":"South Pacific (1958 film)","blocks":[{"text":"South Pacific is a 1958 American romantic musical film directed by Joshua Logan, based on the 1949 Rodgers and Hammerstein stage musical, which in turn was based on James A. Michener's 1947 short-story collection Tales of the South Pacific. It stars Rossano Brazzi, Mitzi Gaynor, John Kerr, Ray Walston, and France Nuyen in the leading roles with Juanita Hall as Bloody Mary, the part that she had played in the original stage production. It is set in 1943, during World War II, on an island in the South Pacific."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/57/Poster_of_the_movie_South_Pacific.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Pacific_(1958_film)"},{"title":"Pacific Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest of Earth's five oceanic divisions. It stretches from the Arctic Ocean in the north to the Southern Ocean, or, depending on the definition, to Antarctica in the south, and is bounded by the continents of Asia and Australia in the west and the Americas in the east."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f6/Pacific_Ocean_-_en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pacific_Ocean"},{"title":"South","blocks":[{"text":"South is one of the cardinal directions or compass points. The direction is the opposite of north and is perpendicular to both west and east."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Compass_Rose_English_South.svg/330px-Compass_Rose_English_South.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/R_and_H.jpg/500px-R_and_H.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/R_and_H.jpg/1280px-R_and_H.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/R_and_H.jpg/500px-R_and_H.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rodgers (left) and Hammerstein","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AR_and_H.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Ezio_Pinza_SP.jpg/250px-Ezio_Pinza_SP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/Ezio_Pinza_SP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.w